9.text:
For a Stokes flow write

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
so that ${\rm div}\,u=0$ gives $W'=-kU$.  With

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu W'\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\,(U'-kW)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the $x-$ and $z-$ momentum equations are

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\,(U''-k^{2}U)=0,\qquad -P'+\mu\,(W''-k^{2}W)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
which with $U=-W'/k$ gives the biharmonic problem

\begin{equation*}
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}W=0\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to an inessential additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=a_{1}\cosh kz+a_{2}\sinh kz+a_{3}z\cosh kz+a_{4}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $U(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
W'(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;a_{3}=-ka_{2}\;,
\qquad W(0)=\dot e(t)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the linearised kinematic condition $W(0)=\dot e$

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=\mu\frac{W'''(0)}{k^{2}}=-2k\mu a_{2}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed normal traction on the deflected top,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=-2k\mu a_{2}=\rho g\,e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
gives the instantaneous relation

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}(t)=\frac{\rho g}{2k\mu}\,e(t)\; ,
\end{equation*}
and $a_{1}(t)=\dot e(t)$.  On the lower side

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow P(h)=2\mu W'(h)\Longleftrightarrow
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow
-\frac{\mu}{k}\!\left[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\right]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $a_{1}=0$ for the time independent state, $\dot e_{s}=0$,
$W(0)=0$, the two lower conditions give

\begin{equation*}
s_{h}\big[-a_{1}+k h\,a_{2}\big]=c_{h}\big[a_{2}+h a_{4}\big],
\end{equation*}
with $c_{h}=\cosh kh$, $s_{h}=\sinh kh$, and

\begin{equation*}
-k a_{2}\frac{(kh)^{2}}{h}c_{h}+a_{4}\!\left[kh\,s_{h}+c_{h}\right]
=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $a_{4}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{a_{2}}{h\,c_{h}}\Big[(kh)^{2}+c_{h}^{2}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{2}= \rho g e_{s}/2k\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g}{k}\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{h\,c_{h}}{(kh)^{2}+c_{h}^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $kh\equiv\kappa$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      \chi(\kappa) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
          =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\kappa}{\cosh\kappa}+\frac{\cosh\kappa}
          {\kappa}}\;, \qquad \kappa\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless amplification of the imposed basal shear.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{1}{\,(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)+\big(\cosh\kappa/\kappa\big)} ,
\end{equation*}
so that for the long wave, thin-plate limit $\kappa=kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh\kappa=1+O(\kappa^{2}),\qquad 
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa}{1+O(\kappa^{2})}\approx kh ,
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The function $\chi(\kappa)$
attains its maximum $\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46$ at $\kappa\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2$,
commonly quoted as $\chi\approx1/2$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx
{\rm const.}\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ dependent reduction
shown above.  With the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ written for the
buoyancy restoring stress the same expression is $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ given above.  This is the usual
$k h$–dependent viscous flexure factor; in the often quoted
$k h\ll1$ limit it reduces to the familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$.

3.text:
In the linear Stokes regime put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=G(z)\cos kx ,\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\partial_{x}u_{x}+\partial_{z}u_{z}=0$ gives $G'=-kF$ and

\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[-k^{2}F+F''\Big] ,\qquad 
G''''-k^{4}G=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the stress free mode,

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
G&=a_{1}\cosh kz+a_{2}\sinh kz+a_{3}\cos kz+a_{4}\sin kz  \\
F&= -a_{1}\sinh kz-a_{2}\cosh kz+a_{3}\sin kz-a_{4}\cos kz .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
The no slip condition at the top $u_{x}(0)=0$ gives $a_{2}=-a_{4}$ and
with the viscous normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}= \big[-P+2\mu G'\big]\cos kx
\end{equation*}
the top condition $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ is

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
2\mu k a_{2}=\rho g e ,
\qquad a_{2}=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e ,           \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$G'(0)=0$ being identically satisfied.  At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
k^{2}F(h)-F''(h)+2kG'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}\sinh kh+a_{2}\cosh kh=0,
\qquad a_{1}=-a_{2}\coth kh  , \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F'(h)-kG(h)\big]\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F'(h)-kG(h)=-2k\big[a_{1}\cosh kh+a_{2}\sinh kh\big],
\end{equation*}
so that with (2)

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}\cosh kh+a_{2}\sinh kh=-\frac{a_{2}}{\sinh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[F'(h)-kG(h)\big]=
\frac{2\mu k a_{2}}{\sinh kh}=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence with (1)

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\sinh kh,\qquad 
\rho g e_{s}=2\mu k a_{2}=S_{0}\sinh kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the instantaneous viscous response

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\sinh kh\,\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the stress free mode $a_{3}$ being the neutral vertical motion which
can be chosen so that $u_{z}(0)=\dot e\cos kx$.  In particular the
amplitude of the isostatic deflection is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\sinh kh}{\rho g}\;S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the usual geodynamic restoring density being $\Delta\rho$.  With the
isostatic normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
written in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi=\sinh kh,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin sheet $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$ and
$e_{s}\simeq S_{0}h/\Delta\rho g$.  In the notation of the statement in
which the top stress is written with the plate density $\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\sinh kh\;\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\sinh kh ,
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\chi=\sinh kh$ on identifying the density which
appears in the top normal stress with the isostatic density
$\Delta\rho$.  This is the familiar $ \sinh kh$ dynamic-topography
response of a viscous channel to a harmonic basal traction; for
$kh\ll1$ $e_{s}\approx S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$.

6.text:
In an incompressible Stokes flow put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\Phi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr]^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\qquad 
P=\mu\Bigl(k\Phi^{\prime}-\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}\Bigr) .
\end{equation*}
With the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the material surface

\begin{equation*}
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=-k\Phi(0) ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the stationary limit $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\bigl(-k pz\bigr)\cosh kz+\bigl(p+qz\bigr)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies the two conditions at $z=0$.  The normal stress at the
top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}
          =-\mu\Bigl[k\Phi^{\prime}-\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}
          +2k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)-2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(0)=\rho ge ,
\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=\rho ge .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\bigr]_{h}\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx,
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $t=k h$, $c=\cosh t$, $s=\sinh t$ and using (1)–(3) with
$\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=\frac{k\rho ge}{\mu},\qquad
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With the above form of $\Phi$ the three conditions give

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k},\qquad 
q=\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k}\,
\frac{c-t s}{c},
\end{equation*}
and (3) becomes

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g e}{\mu}\Bigl[\frac{t}{c}+\frac{c}{t}\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{1}{\displaystyle \frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}} ,
\qquad t=k h .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}}
      =\frac{t\cosh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that in particular

$$
\chi=\Bigl[\,k h\,{\rm sech}(k h)+{1\over k h}\cosh(k h)\Bigr]^{-1},
$$

the familiar small–wavelength limit $\chi\simeq k h$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\simeq k h\,e^{-k h}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.
With the notation $\rho_{p}$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho_{p}ge\cos kx$ the
same result is $e_{s}=S_{0}/\rho_{p}g\,\chi$, i.e. $\chi_{p}=\chi$.
For long waves $\chi\simeq k h$ and $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the usual shear-driven dynamic topography.  This is the conventional
writing $e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\,[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$, up to the
obvious $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification.

2.text:
The stream function can be put in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi (z,x)=\Psi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Psi^{\prime}\sin kx ,\; 
w=-k\Psi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $t=k h$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi''''-2k^{2}\Psi''+k^{4}\Psi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Psi=0$.  With the top conditions
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $w(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the two dimensionally independent solutions can be taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{1}(z)=2\sinh kz-2kz\,e^{-kz},\qquad 
\Psi_{2}(z)=2z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Psi=A\Psi_{1}+B\Psi_{2}$ 

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad 
P_{0}(z)=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big[\Psi'''(z)-k^{2}\Psi'(z)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P_{0}+2\eta k\Psi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence at the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)\cos kx
        =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\eta}{k\rho g}\,\Psi'''(0)
      =-\,\frac{4k^{2}\eta}{\rho g}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}\sin kx
        =S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h),
\end{equation*}
which with the two basis functions gives

\begin{equation*}
Ak\big[\cosh t+t e^{-t}\big]+B\,t\cosh t=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
kA\Big[\sinh t+e^{-t}(1-t)\Big]+B\big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\big]
     =\frac{S_{0}}{4k\eta}\;,\qquad t=k h .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$,

\begin{equation*}
A=-\,\frac{S_{0}\,t\cosh t}{4k^{2}\eta\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]} ,
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=k h ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic density contrast $\Delta\rho$ in place of
$\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(k h)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{t\,\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\;, 
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the familiar long–wave notation

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{(k h)\cosh(k h)}{\cosh^{2}(k h)+(k h)^{2}}\; .
\]

In particular for $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\[
\chi(t)\simeq \frac{t}{1+t^{2}},\qquad e_{s}\simeq 
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\frac{k h}{1+(k h)^{2}}\;,
\]

which is the usual viscous-channel dynamic topography.  The
dimensionless factor has the absolute maximum

\[
\max_{t>0}\chi(t)\simeq0.46\approx{\tfrac12},
\]

attained for $t=k h\sim1$ and often quoted as the most unstable,
i.e. the ‘stable’ , long-wave limit $\chi_{\max}\approx1/2$,

\[
e_{s}^{\max}\;\approx\;\frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\]

which is the $kh\approx1$ form of the exact result
$\chi(t)=t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$, viz. $e_{s}\approx
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi\approx k h$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow
\Delta\rho$ identification this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi(t)$ above; the often quoted numerical
value is $\chi\approx1/2$ at the most responsive wavelength,
$k h\approx1$, i.e. $e_{s}\simeq\frac12S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
maximum–growth limit.  This is the $k h\to0$ long-wave limit
$\chi\simeq kh$ usually written as $\chi\approx{\rm const}\approx
1/2$ for the most unstable mode.

public_text.9:
For a Stokes flow write

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;, \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
so that ${\rm div}\,u=0$ gives $W'=-kU$.  With

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu W'\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\,(U'-kW)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the $x-$ and $z-$ momentum equations are

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\,(U''-k^{2}U)=0,\qquad -P'+\mu\,(W''-k^{2}W)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
which with $U=-W'/k$ gives the biharmonic problem

\begin{equation*}
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}W=0\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to an inessential additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=a_{1}\cosh kz+a_{2}\sinh kz+a_{3}z\cosh kz+a_{4}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $U(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
W'(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;a_{3}=-ka_{2}\;,
\qquad W(0)=\dot e(t)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the linearised kinematic condition $W(0)=\dot e$

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=\mu\frac{W'''(0)}{k^{2}}=-2k\mu a_{2}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed normal traction on the deflected top,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=-2k\mu a_{2}=\rho g\,e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
gives the instantaneous relation

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}(t)=\frac{\rho g}{2k\mu}\,e(t)\; ,
\end{equation*}
and $a_{1}(t)=\dot e(t)$.  On the lower side

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow P(h)=2\mu W'(h)\Longleftrightarrow
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow
-\frac{\mu}{k}\!\left[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\right]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $a_{1}=0$ for the time independent state, $\dot e_{s}=0$,
$W(0)=0$, the two lower conditions give

\begin{equation*}
s_{h}\big[-a_{1}+k h\,a_{2}\big]=c_{h}\big[a_{2}+h a_{4}\big],
\end{equation*}
with $c_{h}=\cosh kh$, $s_{h}=\sinh kh$, and

\begin{equation*}
-k a_{2}\frac{(kh)^{2}}{h}c_{h}+a_{4}\!\left[kh\,s_{h}+c_{h}\right]
=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $a_{4}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{a_{2}}{h\,c_{h}}\Big[(kh)^{2}+c_{h}^{2}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{2}= \rho g e_{s}/2k\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g}{k}\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{h\,c_{h}}{(kh)^{2}+c_{h}^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $kh\equiv\kappa$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      \chi(\kappa) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
          =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\kappa}{\cosh\kappa}+\frac{\cosh\kappa}
          {\kappa}}\;, \qquad \kappa\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless amplification of the imposed basal shear.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{1}{\,(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)+\big(\cosh\kappa/\kappa\big)} ,
\end{equation*}
so that for the long wave, thin-plate limit $\kappa=kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh\kappa=1+O(\kappa^{2}),\qquad 
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa}{1+O(\kappa^{2})}\approx kh ,
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The function $\chi(\kappa)$
attains its maximum $\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46$ at $\kappa\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2$,
commonly quoted as $\chi\approx1/2$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx
{\rm const.}\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ dependent reduction
shown above.  With the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ written for the
buoyancy restoring stress the same expression is $e_{s}= \chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ given above.  This is the usual
$k h$–dependent viscous flexure factor; in the often quoted
$k h\ll1$ limit it reduces to the familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$.

public_text.3:
