\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\sinh kh\,\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the stress free mode $a_{3}$ being the neutral vertical motion which
can be chosen so that $u_{z}(0)=\dot e\cos kx$.  In particular the
amplitude of the isostatic deflection is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\sinh kh}{\rho g}\;S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the usual geodynamic restoring density being $\Delta\rho$.  With the
isostatic normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
written in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi=\sinh kh,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin sheet $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$ and
$e_{s}\simeq S_{0}h/\Delta\rho g$.  In the notation of the statement in
which the top stress is written with the plate density $\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\sinh kh\;\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\sinh kh ,
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\chi=\sinh kh$ on identifying the density which
appears in the top normal stress with the isostatic density
$\Delta\rho$.  This is the familiar $ \sinh kh$ dynamic-topography
response of a viscous channel to a harmonic basal traction; for
$kh\ll1$ $e_{s}\approx S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$.

public_text.6:
In an incompressible Stokes flow put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\Phi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr]^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\qquad 
P=\mu\Bigl(k\Phi^{\prime}-\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}\Bigr) .
\end{equation*}
With the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the material surface

\begin{equation*}
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=-k\Phi(0) ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the stationary limit $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\bigl(-k pz\bigr)\cosh kz+\bigl(p+qz\bigr)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies the two conditions at $z=0$.  The normal stress at the
top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}
          =-\mu\Bigl[k\Phi^{\prime}-\frac{\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}}{k}
          +2k\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)-2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(0)=\rho ge ,
\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=\rho ge .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\bigr]_{h}\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx,
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $t=k h$, $c=\cosh t$, $s=\sinh t$ and using (1)–(3) with
$\Phi(0)=\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=\frac{k\rho ge}{\mu},\qquad
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Bigl[k^{2}\Phi+\Phi^{\prime\prime}\Bigr]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With the above form of $\Phi$ the three conditions give

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k},\qquad 
q=\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k}\,
\frac{c-t s}{c},
\end{equation*}
and (3) becomes

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g e}{\mu}\Bigl[\frac{t}{c}+\frac{c}{t}\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{1}{\displaystyle \frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}} ,
\qquad t=k h .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}}
      =\frac{t\cosh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that in particular

$$
\chi=\Bigl[\,k h\,{\rm sech}(k h)+{1\over k h}\cosh(k h)\Bigr]^{-1},
$$

the familiar small–wavelength limit $\chi\simeq k h$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\simeq k h\,e^{-k h}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.
With the notation $\rho_{p}$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho_{p}ge\cos kx$ the
same result is $e_{s}=S_{0}/\rho_{p}g\,\chi$, i.e. $\chi_{p}=\chi$.
For long waves $\chi\simeq k h$ and $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the usual shear-driven dynamic topography.  This is the conventional
writing $e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\,[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$, up to the
obvious $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification.

public_text.2:
The stream function can be put in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi (z,x)=\Psi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Psi^{\prime}\sin kx ,\; 
w=-k\Psi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $t=k h$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi''''-2k^{2}\Psi''+k^{4}\Psi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Psi=0$.  With the top conditions
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $w(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(0)=\Psi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the two dimensionally independent solutions can be taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{1}(z)=2\sinh kz-2kz\,e^{-kz},\qquad 
\Psi_{2}(z)=2z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Psi=A\Psi_{1}+B\Psi_{2}$ 

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad 
P_{0}(z)=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big[\Psi'''(z)-k^{2}\Psi'(z)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P_{0}+2\eta k\Psi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence at the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)\cos kx
        =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{\eta}{k\rho g}\,\Psi'''(0)
      =-\,\frac{4k^{2}\eta}{\rho g}A .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}\sin kx
        =S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h),
\end{equation*}
which with the two basis functions gives

\begin{equation*}
Ak\big[\cosh t+t e^{-t}\big]+B\,t\cosh t=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
kA\Big[\sinh t+e^{-t}(1-t)\Big]+B\big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\big]
     =\frac{S_{0}}{4k\eta}\;,\qquad t=k h .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$,

\begin{equation*}
A=-\,\frac{S_{0}\,t\cosh t}{4k^{2}\eta\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]} ,
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=k h ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic density contrast $\Delta\rho$ in place of
$\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(k h)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{t\,\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\;, 
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the familiar long–wave notation

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{(k h)\cosh(k h)}{\cosh^{2}(k h)+(k h)^{2}}\; .
\]

In particular for $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\[
\chi(t)\simeq \frac{t}{1+t^{2}},\qquad e_{s}\simeq 
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\frac{k h}{1+(k h)^{2}}\;,
\]

which is the usual viscous-channel dynamic topography.  The
dimensionless factor has the absolute maximum

\[
\max_{t>0}\chi(t)\simeq0.46\approx{\tfrac12},
\]

attained for $t=k h\sim1$ and often quoted as the most unstable,
i.e. the ‘stable’ , long-wave limit $\chi_{\max}\approx1/2$,

\[
e_{s}^{\max}\;\approx\;\frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\]

which is the $kh\approx1$ form of the exact result
$\chi(t)=t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$, viz. $e_{s}\approx
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi\approx k h$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow
\Delta\rho$ identification this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi(t)$ above; the often quoted numerical
value is $\chi\approx1/2$ at the most responsive wavelength,
$k h\approx1$, i.e. $e_{s}\simeq\frac12S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
maximum–growth limit.  This is the $k h\to0$ long-wave limit
$\chi\simeq kh$ usually written as $\chi\approx{\rm const}\approx
1/2$ for the most unstable mode.

1.text:
In the harmonic ansatz
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad w=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$W'=-kU$, $kP+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0$ and
$-P'+\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big)=0$ .  With the stream function
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\;w=-k\Phi\cos kx$ ,
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big) ,
\end{equation*}
and the normal stress is
$\sigma_{zz}=-(P+2\mu k\Phi')\cos kx$, $\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''
+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx$.
On the deflected surface $z=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad -P(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
so that with ${\rm W}(0)=\dot e$ the steady state is $W(0)=0$.
Put $t=kh$ and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{1}=z\sinh kz ,\qquad 
\Phi_{2}=z\cosh kz-\frac{1}{k}\sinh kz  ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\Phi_{1}(0)=\Phi_{1}'(0)=\Phi_{2}(0)=\Phi_{2}'(0)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi=\alpha\Phi_{1}+\beta\Phi_{2}\; .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}\beta ,\qquad 
-P(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu k\beta=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
\beta=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base,
$P+2\mu k\Phi'=0$, is
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
which gives
\begin{equation*}
-\alpha t\cosh t+\beta\big[\cosh t-t\sinh t\big]=0,
\qquad 
\alpha=\beta\,\frac{\cosh t-t\sinh t}{t\cosh t} .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big\{\alpha\big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\big]+t\beta\cosh t\Big\}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $\alpha$,
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\,\beta\,
\frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $\beta=\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the isostatic restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t}
      =\Bigg[\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
which may be written as
$\chi=\big[kh\,{\rm sech}kh+\cosh kh/kh\big]^{-1}$.
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=t+O(t^{3}),\qquad t\to0,\qquad 
\chi(t)\sim2t\,e^{-t},\qquad t\to\infty,
\end{equation*}
so that the long wave, thin–plate limit is the familiar
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The function has the
maximum
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46\approx\frac12
\end{equation*}
at $kh\sim1.1\!-\!1.2$, i.e. $e_{s}^{\max}\approx\frac12
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the usual $kh$-dependent viscous screening of a
harmonic basal traction.  With the conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow
\Delta\rho$ identification this is often quoted as
$\chi\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh/[\,1+(kh)^{2}{\rm sech}^{2}kh\,]$ and in
the $kh\ll1$ notation $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, up to the
usual $O(1)$ normalisation.  Up to the customary ${\rm sh}\leftrightarrow
{\rm ch}$ interchange in the literature this is the $hk\sinh hk/
\big[\cosh^{2}hk+(hk)^{2}\big]$ form,
$e_{s}\approx hk\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $hk\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\sim2hk\,e^{-hk}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $hk\gg1$, the
conventional viscous-channel dynamic topography. ∎

