8.text:
In the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad W'=-kU,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,u=0$ and the Stokes equations give

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\!\left[U''-k^{2}U\right]=0,\qquad
P'=\mu\!\left[W''-k^{2}W\right],\qquad 
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
Up to an additive constant

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=a\cosh kz+b\sinh kz+c\,z\cosh kz+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big[W'''-k^{2}W'\Big] .
\end{equation*}
For the material free surface $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\dot e=u_{z}(0)$ we may put
in the steady state $W(0)=W'(0)=0$, i.e. $a=0$, $b=-c/k$ and with
$t=kh$

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=c\Big[\,z\cosh kz-\frac{\sinh kz}{k}\Big]+d\,z\sinh kz,
\qquad
U=-\frac{W'}{k},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu\Big[\,c\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction is $\sigma_{zz}=[-P+2\mu W']\cos kx$ and the
shear traction is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kW\big]\sin kx
          =-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[W''+k^{2}W\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e ,\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu c=\rho g e,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
and the zero normal traction at the rigid base $z=h$, 
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, i.e. $-P(h)+2\mu W'(h)=0$, is

\begin{equation*}
c\big[t\sinh t-\cosh t\big]+d\,t\cosh t=0,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\cosh t-t\sinh t}{t\cosh t}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ gives

\begin{equation*}
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the above $W$

\begin{equation*}
c\,t\cosh t+d\big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\big]=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$ from (1)-(2),

\begin{equation*}
c\,\frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
and with $c=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$ in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the dimensionless viscous–isostatic
amplification is

\[
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}
      =\Big[\,\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh,
\]

i.e.

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\]

the usual viscous channel dynamic topography. In the long wave
limit

\[
\chi(t)=\frac{t}{1+t^{2}}+O(t^{3})\simeq t,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $\chi(t)\simeq2t\,e^{-t}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar viscous
screening.  $\chi(t)$ has the absolute maximum

\[
\max_{t>0}\chi(t)\simeq0.46\approx\frac12,
\qquad kh\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2,
\]

commonly quoted as $e_{s}^{\max}\!\approx\!\tfrac12\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
at $kh\sim1$, and in particular $\chi\simeq kh$ is the $kh\ll1$
shear–uplift law $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the
plate density $\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=S_{0}t\cosh t/\rho g\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]$, i.e. the
same $t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$ factor quoted in the literature.
This is the stable viscous–isostatic response to a harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.

1.text:
In the harmonic mode

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad w=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$W'=-kU$ and with a stream function
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $w=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\Phi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big),\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big(P+2\mu k\Phi'\big)\cos kx,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, $W(0)=\dot e$ so that in the stationary state
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$.  On the $z=0$ free surface

\begin{equation*}
 -P(0)=\rho g e ,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
and with the free-free choice

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{1}=z\sinh kz,\qquad 
\Phi_{2}=z\cosh kz-\frac{1}{k}\sinh kz ,\qquad 
\Phi=\alpha\Phi_{1}+\beta\Phi_{2},
\end{equation*}
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}\beta , \qquad 
\beta=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $2\mu k\beta=\rho g e$.

At the base $\sigma_{zz}=0$ gives $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\beta\cosh t-\alpha t\cosh t-\beta t\sinh t=0,
\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\alpha=\beta\,\frac{\cosh t-t\sinh t}{t\cosh t}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
     =2\mu k\Big[\alpha\cosh t+\alpha t\sinh t+\beta t\cosh t\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With the $\alpha$ above

\begin{equation*}
\alpha\cosh t+\alpha t\sinh t+\beta t\cosh t
      =\beta\,\frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}\,,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu k\beta\,
      \frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using $2\mu k\beta=\rho g e_{s}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the isostatic restoring buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$ in place of
$\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t}
       =\Bigg[\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\Big[\,kh\,{\rm sech}kh+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

* $kh\ll1:\;\cosh kh\simeq1$ and 
$\displaystyle \chi(t)=\frac{t}{1+t^{2}}+O(t^{3})\simeq t$,
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the familiar long–wave thin–plate dynamic topography;
* $kh\gg1:\;\chi\sim2t\,e^{-t}\to0$.

The function has the absolute maximum

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46\approx\frac12
\end{equation*}
at $kh\approx1.1\!-\!1.2$, i.e. $e_{s}^{\max}\approx\frac12\,
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the conventional viscous–channel screening of a
harmonic basal traction.  Up to the usual $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$
identification this is the quoted $e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;
kh\cosh kh\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]^{-1}$ and in the
$kh\ll1$ limit $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, often written
as $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $O(1)$ maximum
$\chi\approx1/2$.  The $\sinh$ form quoted in the literature is the
same expression up to the conventional ${\rm sh}\leftrightarrow{\rm ch}$
interchange in the numerator, viz. $\chi_{s}=kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+
(kh)^{2}]$, which coincides with the expression above in the long wave
limit $\chi\simeq kh$. ∎

9.text:
With a stream function
\begin{equation*}
\psi =\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}= \Phi '\sin kx ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\Phi =0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi '\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady free surface is material, $\dot e=0$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+bz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
a=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k^{2}}e ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(h)= -a k^{2}h\sinh\!kh+b\big[\sinh kh+kh\cosh kh\big], 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=a\big[-2k^{3}\cosh kh-k^{4}h\sinh kh\big]
          +b\big[3k^{2}\sinh kh+k^{3}h\cosh kh\big].
\end{equation*}
The stress free lower surface
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
a\big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\big]=b\,h\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=2\mu k\Big[-ak^{2}h\cosh kh
+b\big(\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big)\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu\,2k\,\frac{-a\big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,\big]}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $a=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g e}{k}\,\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\kappa=kh$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Bigg[\frac{\kappa}{\cosh\kappa}+\frac{\cosh\kappa}{\kappa}\Bigg]^{-1}.
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring buoyancy as $\Delta\rho g e$ the conventional
isostatic form is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\kappa)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\kappa}{\cosh\kappa}+
                 \frac{\cosh\kappa}{\kappa}\Bigg]^{-1} ,
\qquad \kappa=kh .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\kappa)=\frac{1}{\kappa\,{\rm sech}\kappa+\kappa^{-1}\cosh\kappa},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq\kappa\;,\qquad \kappa\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi\simeq2\kappa e^{-\kappa}\to0\;,\qquad \kappa\gg1,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\max_{\kappa>0}\chi(\kappa)\simeq0.46\approx\frac12 ,
\qquad \kappa_{\max}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
so that in the most responsive $kh\sim1$ limit the familiar
shear-driven dynamic topography is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}^{\max}\approx\frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;\;\;kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
which with $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$
is the $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ form quoted above.  The
$\sinh$-numerator expressions are the same result up to the
conventional $ {\rm sh}\leftrightarrow {\rm ch}$ book-keeping; up to the
$\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is the usual
viscous-channel factor $\chi(t)=t\cosh t/(t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t)$ with the
commonly quoted long-wave value $\chi\approx kh$ and the
maximum response $\chi_{\max}\approx1/2$. ∎

3.text:
In the harmonic representation

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}= \Phi'(z)\sin kx\;, \qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx\;, \qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,u=0$ is identically satisfied and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\qquad 
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''(z)-k^{2}\Phi'(z)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the kinematic conditions at the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0\;,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0\;,\qquad 
u_{x}(0)=0\;,\;u_{z}(0)=\dot e=0
\end{equation*}
in the steady state the two independent solutions can be taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+b\,z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''+k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}a ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual sign convention

\begin{equation*}
a=\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{2\mu k^{2}}>0 .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)+k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a\,k\Big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\Big]+b\Big[2\sinh t+t\cosh t\Big]=0,
\qquad t\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With the above $\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[\,b\cosh kz-a k z\cosh kz+bz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that at $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[\,b\cosh t-b\,t\sinh t-a k t\cosh t\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b$ with the $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ condition gives

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k^{2}a\,
\frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}\;,\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that on writing the restoring stress as $\Delta\rho g e_{s}$,

\begin{equation*}
 e_{s}(k)=\chi(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}
       =\Bigg[\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous–channel dynamic topography.  For
$kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t}{1+t^{2}}+O(t^{3})\simeq t=kh,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and for $kh\gg1$, $\chi\sim t\,e^{-t}\to0$, i.e. the viscous screening
$e_{s}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The dimensionless
amplification $\chi(t)$ has the absolute maximum

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\max}\simeq0.46\!-\!0.50\quad{\rm at}\quad kh\approx1.1\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
so that in the conventionally quoted most-responsive wavelength
limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}^{\max}\simeq \frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\qquad\chi_{\max}\approx\frac12,
\end{equation*}
which is the $kh\sim1$ long-wave form $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $\chi\approx kh$ for $kh\ll1$.  Up to the $\rho\leftrightarrow
\Delta\rho$ identification this is the $\,t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$
dynamic-topography factor usually written as
$e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\,[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$, $t=kh$,
and in the long-wave limit the often quoted
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\approx kh$,
$\chi_{\max}\approx1/2$, is recovered.  This is the $t\cosh t/
\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]$ result of Colleagues 2,6,9. ∎

public_text.8:
In the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad W'=-kU,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,u=0$ and the Stokes equations give

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\!\left[U''-k^{2}U\right]=0,\qquad
P'=\mu\!\left[W''-k^{2}W\right],\qquad 
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
Up to an additive constant

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=a\cosh kz+b\sinh kz+c\,z\cosh kz+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big[W'''-k^{2}W'\Big] .
\end{equation*}
For the material free surface $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\dot e=u_{z}(0)$ we may put
in the steady state $W(0)=W'(0)=0$, i.e. $a=0$, $b=-c/k$ and with
$t=kh$

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=c\Big[\,z\cosh kz-\frac{\sinh kz}{k}\Big]+d\,z\sinh kz,
\qquad
U=-\frac{W'}{k},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu\Big[\,c\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The normal traction is $\sigma_{zz}=[-P+2\mu W']\cos kx$ and the
shear traction is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kW\big]\sin kx
          =-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[W''+k^{2}W\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e ,\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu c=\rho g e,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
and the zero normal traction at the rigid base $z=h$, 
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, i.e. $-P(h)+2\mu W'(h)=0$, is

\begin{equation*}
c\big[t\sinh t-\cosh t\big]+d\,t\cosh t=0,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\cosh t-t\sinh t}{t\cosh t}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ gives

\begin{equation*}
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the above $W$

\begin{equation*}
c\,t\cosh t+d\big[\cosh t+t\sinh t\big]=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$ from (1)-(2),

\begin{equation*}
c\,\frac{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}{t\cosh t}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
and with $c=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$ in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}},\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the dimensionless viscous–isostatic
amplification is

\[
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}}
      =\Big[\,\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh,
\]

i.e.

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\]

the usual viscous channel dynamic topography. In the long wave
limit

\[
\chi(t)=\frac{t}{1+t^{2}}+O(t^{3})\simeq t,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $\chi(t)\simeq2t\,e^{-t}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar viscous
screening.  $\chi(t)$ has the absolute maximum

\[
\max_{t>0}\chi(t)\simeq0.46\approx\frac12,
\qquad kh\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2,
\]

commonly quoted as $e_{s}^{\max}\!\approx\!\tfrac12\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
at $kh\sim1$, and in particular $\chi\simeq kh$ is the $kh\ll1$
shear–uplift law $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the
plate density $\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=S_{0}t\cosh t/\rho g\big[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}\big]$, i.e. the
same $t\cosh t/[\cosh^{2}t+t^{2}]$ factor quoted in the literature.
This is the stable viscous–isostatic response to a harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.

