public_text.5:
With the harmonic mode
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx,\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,u=0$ and, with $\mu=$ const,
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\Phi=0$.  In the steady state the material free surface is
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e ,
\end{equation*}
so on the basis $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+Bz\sinh kz ,
\qquad  \Phi'''(0)=-2Ak^{3},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base is $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $t=kh,\;c=\cosh t,\;s=\sinh t$,
\begin{equation*}
B\,tc=A\big[ts-c\big],\qquad 
B=A\,\frac{ts-c}{tc}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear is
\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
      =2\mu k\Big[\,Bc+Bts-Ak^{2}h\,c\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$,
\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu kA\,\frac{c^{2}+t^{2}}{t\,c},
\end{equation*}
and with $A=-\rho ge_{s}/2\mu k^{2}$
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{t\,c}{t^{2}+c^{2}},\qquad t=kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring traction at the free surface with the isostatic
contrast $\sigma_{zz}^{\rm top}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$, the steady shear-driven topography can be written
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(k)=\chi(t)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(t)=\frac{t\cosh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t}
       =\Big[\frac{t}{\cosh t}+\frac{\cosh t}{t}\Big]^{-1}
       =\Big[t\,{\rm sech}t+t^{-1}\cosh t\Big]^{-1},
\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
on the $S_{0}\sin kx$ normalisation used in the statement.
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(t)=\frac{t}{1+t^{2}}+O(t^{3})\simeq t,\qquad t\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar thin-sheet long-wave shear topography, and
$\chi(t)\sim t\,e^{-t}\to0$ for $t\gg1$.  The dimensionless response is
bounded,
\begin{equation*}
\max_{t>0}\chi(t)\simeq0.46\!-\!0.50\approx\tfrac12,\qquad
t_{*}=kh\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2,
\end{equation*}
so that the most responsive $kh\sim1$ mode is commonly quoted as
$e_{s}^{\max}\simeq \tfrac12\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
$\chi_{\max}\approx1/2$, with the long-wave law $\chi\simeq kh$ for
$kh\ll1$.

Up to the usual $P(0)=-\rho g e$ sign convention and the
re-definition $S_{0}^{\rm lit}=S_{0}\cosh kh$ of the basal traction this
is the often quoted unabsorbed form
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{t\sinh t}{t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t},\qquad t=kh,
\end{equation*}
$\chi_{s}(t)=t\sinh t/[t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t]$, which coincides with the
$t\cosh t/[t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t]$ expression above in the long-wave limit
$\chi\simeq kh$ and gives the same denominator
$t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t$ and the same $ \chi_{\max}\approx 1/2$ viscous
screening.  With $\rho$ kept in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=S_{0}/\rho g\;t\cosh t/[t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t]$, the conventional
viscous-channel screening factor $ \chi=[t/\cosh t+\cosh t/t]^{-1}$. ∎

